<p>nVidia are making a lot of money right now selling GPUs to AI companies.  Why?</p>
<p>Because, we are told, they operate in parallel, unlike CPUs, which are serial.</p>
<p>I don't know very much about AI, but I claim this is a lie.  And possibly one that is holding AI back and making it more expensive and more power-hungry than it needs to be.</p>
<p>GPUs are <i>more</i> parallel than CPUs, because they run the same, very simple, programs over and over again on big datasets, so it makes sense to parallelize that 200, 2000, whatever times.</p>
<p>But they are still, basically, sequential programs, deliberately made that way by the people who designed the architecture of the first computers back in the 1940s.  John von Neumann usually gets the blame, but having been to Bletchley Park, I know that Tommy Flowers put a (very cunning) clock into Colossus in 1942 or 1943.  I have the impression that he already knew that this was "the way forward" when building valve switching circuits in the 1930s.</p>
<p>And it's the <i>clock</i> in computer circuitry that turns a wild-west free-for-all of parallel signals into something sequential <i>that humans can cope with</i>.  That's right: computers are designed the way they are, with all their inefficiencies, so that <i>we</i> can understand them; figure them out when they go wrong and program them.  Human (and animal) brains do not work this way.  Look as long as you want for a sequential clock in a pigeon brain and you won't find it.</p>
<p>Backtracking for a moment for those who might be lost right now: electronic circuitry is basically all parallel.  Components are connected by wires, and in any given wire, or in any given component, there is a "signal" which is transmitted by the voltage in that wire relative to an arbitrary "ground" voltage.  At any given moment, all the wires have their own independent voltages.  All fine.  Most "electrical" things around your house will work this way (anything from toasters to amplifiers).</p>
<p>But it starts to be a problem because engineers don't like  <a href='https://www.allelcoelec.com/blog/sr-flip-flop-knowledge-guide-working-principle,advantages,disadvantages,truth-table,and-differences.html#t5'>invalid states and race conditions</a>.  When you build a circuit that it supposed to "store" something, and then you use it, as you try and store a value, that value is used in your calculation, and everything goes to pot.</p>
<p>There's a very simple reason for this: the computers that we build <i>today</i> are all built on a "mechanical" model.  They are designed to do <i>exactly what we tell them</i> and we want to be very precise about this (down to I want to represent this integer by exactly this number of bits).</p>
<p>Human and animal brains are not built that way.  Let's face it, they weren't designed at all.  For example,  <a href='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blind_Watchmaker'>The Blind Watchmaker</a> by Richard Dawkins, makes the point that, at every step, "brains" developed from something simpler, which did its job.  The "improvement" did much the same job, but slightly better.  Even the existence of a "brain" is just a nervous system (itself a radical "invention") on steroids.</p>
<p>They work because they work.  If they don't work, there's really not much you can do to diagnose it or fix it (ask any psychologist).  Often, if they are damaged, the host dies.  But they are massively parallel?</p>
<p>How do they not have the same "invalid states" problem then?  Because the human brain <i>is not trying to store values</i>.  In fact, your personal experience <i>probably</i> tells you that your own human brain is really bad at this (how many phone numbers can you remember?  compared to say  <a href='https://www.411.com/'>411.com</a>?).  So "invalid states" are not a problem because precision is not a problem because this is not what the human brain is designed for.  The human brain is just a piece of self-modifying circuitry that attempts to come to a "steady state".  And then when it does, all the operations that it wants to perform are based on that state.</p>
<p>Again, I don't really know much about AI in general, and certainly not about LLMs, but I have been around people doing AI on and off for over 30 years now.  And I know this much: if you want to simulate a human brain with a neural network, you don't need a clock, because the human brain doesn't have a clock and if you're using a clock you're doing it wrong.  No, I don't know how to do it right, and I'm not judging you for not knowing, but I want you to accept that you are doing it wrong, because sooner or later someone smarter than me (and who is working on AI) will figure out how to do it right and they will undercut you and nVidia by factors of 1000s.</p>
<p>No, I'm not making that up for shock value.  I really believe that we are talking about those kind of numbers.</p>
<p>It's really very simple: we have built up all this technology so that we can build systems that <i>do what we tell them to do</i>.  And that involves being very inefficient with all the gates that we use so that we can perform a twenty step operation repeatedly.  And then we use that circuitry to try and replicate a system <i>that does not have a clock</i>.</p>
<p>Think about it.</p>
<p>I think there is a very much simpler, cheaper, less power-hungry chip "out there", waiting to be built, which <i>does not have a CLK input</i>.  Let's call it a "neuron" gate.  It has 1000 connections to other neuron gates.  You can maybe pack 10 billion of them onto a chip and add 1000 external connections to other chips.  And pack 100 of them onto a board and put them in a cabinet.  And then run it on your training data.</p>
<p>Go on, tell me I'm wrong.</p>
